2. Business Objective and Scope
The objective is to convert transactional marketplace data into evidence that can support commercial and operational decisions. The analysis is organised around five business areas:
•	Sales performance: delivered item revenue, freight, monthly trends and geographic contribution.
•	Customer behaviour: customer spend, repeat purchasing and average order value by city.
•	Product performance: category unit volume and product revenue rankings within categories.
•	Seller and payment performance: seller revenue contribution and payment-type activity.
•	Operational experience: orders without line items, review scores and delivery punctuality.
3. Dataset and Data Model
The project uses the Olist public relational dataset. The working database is named olist_ecommerce and contains the following tables:
Table	Analytical role
customers	Customer and customer-city attributes; customer_unique_id identifies a person across orders.
orders	Order-level status and purchase/delivery timestamps.
order_items	Product, seller, item price and freight at order-line level.
payments	Payment method, payment value and instalment information.
products	Product attributes and category identifiers.
sellers	Seller identifiers and seller-city attributes.
reviews	Customer review scores linked to orders.
category_translation	Maps Portuguese category names to English labels.
The dataset does not contain a customer registration date. For Q05, the first recorded order date is used as a proxy; it should not be described as the customer's signup date.
4. Analytical Methodology
•	Loaded and queried the relational dataset in MySQL 8.0.
•	Joined orders to order_items for item-level sales analysis and filtered to delivered orders where revenue was required.
•	Aggregated line items to order or customer level before calculating order/customer metrics, avoiding row multiplication where relevant.
•	Used customer_unique_id for customer-level analysis because customer_id is order-specific in this dataset.
•	Used window functions to rank products within categories and calculate month-over-month revenue movement.
•	Created a monthly_sales_summary view for reusable monthly reporting and a get_customer_history stored procedure for customer-level order history.
Metric definitions
Metric	Definition used
Item revenue	SUM(order_items.price) for delivered orders; excludes freight.
Freight	SUM(order_items.freight_value) for delivered orders; reported separately.
Average order value	Average delivered order item-price total; freight excluded.
Units sold	Count of delivered order-item rows.
Late delivery	Delivered customer date later than estimated delivery date.
Average delivery time	DATEDIFF(customer delivery date, purchase timestamp), in calendar days.
5. Key Findings
5.1 Sales and payment reconciliation
The delivered-order analysis reports R$1,322,149.81 in item revenue and R$219,827.65 in freight. Payments associated with delivered orders total R$1,542,246.77. The payment total is close to the combined item revenue and freight total, but differs by R$269.31. This should be treated as a small reconciliation difference to investigate rather than assumed to be an error.
5.2 Product-category demand
Rank	Category	Delivered units
1	bed_bath_table	10,953
2	health_beauty	9,465
3	sports_leisure	8,431
Bed and bath table products lead the unit-volume result, followed by health and beauty and sports and leisure. These are volume leaders, not necessarily the highest-margin categories; margin data is not available in this analysis.
5.3 Customer purchasing behaviour
The repeat-customer query identifies customers with more than two orders. The highest observed customer order count in the result is 17. The project uses customer_unique_id to avoid treating each order-specific customer_id as a separate person.
5.4 Seller contribution
The seller analysis identifies 34 sellers with delivered item revenue above R$10,000. The leading seller in the captured result generated R$226,987.93 in delivered item revenue. Seller revenue is not the same as seller profit because costs, commissions and returns are not included.
5.5 Delivery performance
The delivery query reports 7,826 late deliveries, representing 8.1% of 96,470 delivered orders with a recorded customer-delivery date. Average delivery time is 12.5 days. The late-delivery percentage is a useful operational KPI, but the analysis does not explain the causes of delay.
5.6 Orders without order items
Order status	Orders without items
Unavailable	603
Canceled	164
Created	5
Invoiced	2
Shipped	1
The 775 orders without matching order-item rows are predominantly unavailable or canceled. This explains why an inner join from orders to order_items excludes these records.
5.7 Customer review scores
Among categories with at least 100 reviews, office_furniture has the lowest average score at 3.65 (1,246 reviews), followed by fashion_male_clothing at 3.82 (105 reviews) and audio at 3.83 (346 reviews). The lower review scores identify categories for further investigation; they do not by themselves establish the cause of dissatisfaction.
6. Recommendations
1.	Prioritise category-level investigation: Review product quality, descriptions, packaging and delivery experience in office_furniture, which has the lowest average review score in the analysed categories.
2.	Monitor delivery punctuality: Track late-delivery rate by seller, customer destination, carrier or region to isolate where the 8.1% late rate is concentrated.
3.	Protect high-volume categories: Use demand planning for bed_bath_table, health_beauty and sports_leisure; validate stock availability and supplier capacity before promotions.
4.	Develop repeat-customer activity: Segment customers by order frequency and spend, then test targeted retention campaigns. Measure repeat purchase rate and incremental revenue.
5.	Review seller concentration: Monitor high-revenue sellers for service levels and dependency risk. Pair revenue reporting with delivery and review KPIs.
6.	Reconcile payment totals: Investigate the R$269.31 difference between delivered-order payments and item revenue plus freight, checking refunds, adjustments and payment allocation.
7. Limitations and Assumptions
•	The dataset is historical and ends on 17 October 2018; findings should not be presented as current marketplace performance.
•	Revenue is defined as delivered item price and excludes freight unless freight is explicitly reported as a separate measure.
•	No product cost, seller cost, commission, marketing spend or margin fields were analysed; profitability cannot be inferred.
•	No customer signup date is available; first purchase date is used only as a proxy in the cohort-style question.
•	Some customer and category analyses use order-level relationships; one review may be associated with multiple categories when an order contains multiple product categories.
•	Delivery-time averages use available timestamps and calendar-day differences; business days and carrier-level causes were not assessed.
•	The payment reconciliation difference requires investigation before the figures are used for financial reporting.
